# Using Codex in {{PROJECT_NAME}}

## What loads automatically

- Root `AGENTS.md` is the project instruction entrypoint.
- `.agents/ai/project-map.json` routes bounded context for discovery tasks.
- Repository skills under `.agents/skills` are discoverable by name and description.
- A full `SKILL.md` loads when explicitly invoked or when its description matches the task.
- Other docs are read on demand through `AGENTS.md`; they are not all loaded for every task.

## Daily workflow

```text
Define the task with docs/TASK_TEMPLATE.md
                ↓
Review and explicitly approve the plan and file set
                ↓
Use a matching project skill or execute the scoped task
                ↓
$build-and-test
                ↓
$code-review
```

### Feature

```text
Define this feature with docs/TASK_TEMPLATE.md and apply GATE-01. Use
$context-discovery when affected files or consumers are not already known.
For every changed source file, use the detected language profile in
docs/CODING_STANDARDS.md. Stop under GATE-02 if implementation exceeds the
approved scope.
```

### Verification

```text
Use $build-and-test. Verify the current diff and report every command as
pass, fail, or not run, including remaining risk.
```

### Review

```text
Use $code-review. Review the current diff and report findings first.
Do not modify files.
```

### Repeated corrections

```text
If the same manual edit or review correction appears in at least three places
or recurs across tasks, record evidence in docs/CODING_RULES_LOG.md. Propose an
approved standards or automation replacement; do not silently change tool
configuration or migrate unrelated historical code.
```

## Project-specific commands

See `docs/VERIFICATION.md`. Model choice is manual; project docs and skills do
not switch the active model. File/byte budgets are deterministic proxies, not
claims about actual token usage.

## What the documents cannot enforce alone

`AGENTS.md` and skills provide durable instructions, but mechanical rules should
also be enforced by the project's confirmed formatters, linters, type checks,
tests, CI, hooks, review approvals, and sandbox/command policy where available.
Adding or changing those controls requires separate task approval.
